@node	0.10, Chapter 1, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Testing and formatting

@noindent
A test in this book is not prose about correctness; it is a program that
computes an actual result and compares it against an expected one, failing
loudly the moment they diverge. This section introduces the two forms this edition uses (Alcotest for pinned examples, QCheck for properties that hold across generated input), using nothing beyond what the last nine sections
already covered: functions, pattern matching, records, and @code{result}.

@subsubheading The smallest function a test can pin

@example
# let add a b = a + b;;
val add : int -> int -> int = <fun>
# add 2 3;;
- : int = 5
@end example

@subsubheading Alcotest: one assertion per example

Alcotest structures a test suite as a list of named groups, each holding a
list of named test cases, each case an ordinary function of type @code{unit
-> unit} whose body raises (through @code{Alcotest.check}) if a computed
value does not match the expected one:

@example
let add_examples () =
  Alcotest.(check int) "add 2 3" 5 (add 2 3)
;;

let () =
  Alcotest.run "sicp_ch0" [ "0.10 testing", [ Alcotest.test_case "add" `Quick add_examples ] ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Alcotest.(check int)} is @code{Alcotest.check Alcotest.int} written
with the local-open shorthand from section 0.3; @code{Alcotest.int} is a
small value (a @newterm{testable}) that tells @code{check} how to compare
two @code{int}s and how to print one if the comparison fails. A testable
exists for most of this book's own types (@code{Alcotest.string}, @code{Alcotest.bool}, @code{Alcotest.list}, @code{Alcotest.option}, @code{Alcotest.result}, @code{Alcotest.float @var{epsilon}} for approximate float comparison), and one can always be built by hand for a type of the
book's own, by pairing an equality function with a printer. Running
@code{Alcotest.run} prints one line per test case, @code{[OK]} or
@code{[FAIL]}, and exits nonzero if anything failed; this is exactly the
executable form @code{dune runtest} runs.

@code{ch0/test/test_replay.ml} and @code{ch0/test/test_solutions.ml}, part
of this edition's own source, are larger examples of the same shape: the
first asserts, section by section, the same results this chapter's
listings show; the second asserts the reference solutions' stated
contracts, exercise by exercise. Their tests are not new material: every value they check has already appeared, verified, in the section that introduced it. They exist so that a change to this chapter's code fails
@code{dune runtest} immediately, rather than only being noticed the next
time someone happens to reread the book.

@subsubheading QCheck: one property, many generated cases

Where Alcotest pins specific input/output pairs, QCheck states a property
that should hold for @emph{every} input from some space, and generates many
inputs to search for a counterexample:

@example
let commutative =
  QCheck.Test.make
    ~name:"add is commutative"
    QCheck.(pair int_small int_small)
    (fun (a, b) -> add a b = add b a)
;;

let () = QCheck_base_runner.run_tests_main [ commutative ]
@end example

@noindent
@code{QCheck.(pair int_small int_small)} describes the space QCheck draws from (pairs of small integers), and the function is the property itself,
returning @code{bool}. QCheck runs the property against a hundred generated
pairs by default, and on failure reports a @emph{shrunk} counterexample: the
smallest failing input QCheck could reduce the original failure to, which is
usually far more informative than whichever large random pair happened to
trigger it first. A property earns its place in this book only when a
plausible bug would actually violate it; @code{add}'s commutativity is deliberately too simple a property to be worth writing a real test for; this
book's later chapters reach for QCheck where an Alcotest-style list of fixed
examples could never be exhaustive: rational-number normalization, a
Huffman-coding round trip, stream-prefix equivalence, and, in chapters 4 and
5, agreement between the evaluator and the compiler on the same program.

@noindent
That listing is illustrative only: @code{dune runtest} does not run it,
because @code{add}'s commutativity is too weak a property to defend anything; the point of the listing is the shape of
a QCheck test, not this particular property. The property this edition
actually runs is @file{ch0/test/prop_folds.ml}, wired into the
@code{runtest} alias like every other executable in this chapter: it states
that the recursive and @code{List.fold_left} forms of exercise 0.2's
@code{sum_cubes} (@ref{0.5}) agree on every generated range, the kind of
agreement a plausible refactor bug could silently break while every pinned
Alcotest example still passes.

@subsubheading Wiring tests into @code{dune runtest}

Every section of this chapter's own library links a small executable that
replays its listings, and every one of those executables is wired into the
@code{runtest} alias with a rule of the same shape:

@example
(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (run ./run_sec_0_10.exe)))
@end example

@noindent
@code{dune runtest} runs the @code{runtest} alias for the whole project:
every rule like the one above, across every chapter, in one pass, and fails
the moment any one of them exits nonzero. Nothing about this rule is
specific to Alcotest or to this chapter's replay executables: @code{(run ./@var{whatever}.exe)} succeeds when its argument exits @code{0} and fails
otherwise, so any program that computes an answer and calls @code{exit 1} on a wrong one can be a test, which is exactly what
@code{Sicp_ch0.Replay.expect} (used throughout this chapter's own listings)
and @code{Alcotest.run} both already do.

@subsubheading @code{dune fmt}

@code{dune fmt} reformats every source file in the project to one
consistent style, driven by the @file{.ocamlformat} file at the project
root (this edition uses the JaneStreet profile at 90 columns). It changes
whitespace and layout only, never behavior, and is safe to run at any
point in development; every listing in this book is itself the output of
@code{dune fmt}, so a reader's own code, formatted the same way, will look
like the book's.

This chapter is now complete: the toolchain, the primitive types, bindings
and functions, pattern matching, lists, records and mutation, closures,
errors, modules as values, and testing. Chapter 1 begins the book itself,
translating SICP's own examples and exercises into exactly this language.
